<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Deceangli</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 48 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of north-east Wales, the first target of Roman campaigns into Wales in 48, whose lead mines soon stamped their name on Roman ingots.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Deceangli lived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flintshire">Flintshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-dee-wales">Dee</a> estuary and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-clwyd">Clwyd</a> valley, a region of hillforts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moel-hiraddug">Moel Hiraddug</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penycloddiau">Penycloddiau</a>. They were probably a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> grouping of local communities and are first named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is known of their rulers or society beyond the archaeology of their hillforts and farms. Their territory was rich in lead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ores on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halkyn-mountain">Halkyn Mountain</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> quickly exploited; lead pigs found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> carry the inscription DECEANGL and a date of 74, showing that mining was organised under the name of the people.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 48 the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-ostorius-scapula">Ostorius Scapula</a> marched against the Deceangli, ravaging their land almost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> before trouble among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/brigantes">Brigantes</a> recalled him. The area was secured by the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> in the 70s, and the Deceangli are not known to have formed a civitas of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/deceangli.jpg" alt="Deceangli" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Rhion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CymruLlwythi.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 48 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deceangi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1&amp;focus=deceangli" aria-label="Deceangli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4650 328 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Deceangli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4970 584h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4970 584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=-1&amp;focus=deceangli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

